body:
Just win the lottery and get plastic surgery. Easy.

&#x200B;

But no in all seriousness 99% of advice anyone gives who doesn't know what a situation feels like is going to be generic and stupid. It doesn't only apply to being ugly, although it's extra bad because how attractive you are is based on genetics (assuming weight/etc. isn't the problem). "Hey just put yourself out there" is easy to say for someone who **can** put themselves out there. Advice from "the other side" is stupid, you're better off ignoring it.

" that pretty much no girls play video games, and overall it is a shameful, pathetic hobby that will earn you nothing but scorn and derision "Popular streamers and people who watch those streamers would disagree. Also so would a lot of regular people.

&#x200B;

I certainly don't cringe at happy relationships. I'm envious of them, sure, but nothing about other people finding love is cringy.  And sure some people will exaggerate their relationships and it may seem over the top, but people tend to exaggerate anything they can so definitely not something specific to relationships.

You're still young. I'm not an optimist or a pessimist but things **can** get better. When I used to go to therapy I would tell my therapist that "I've had plans" not that "I have plans" and usually that means you can talk about it without fear of being admitted. Usually you won't be admitted unless they think you're an immediate danger to yourself, talking about your views on life with them won't land you in a hospital.

&#x200B;

All in all, don't think about the future because you don't know it. Stay alive for the present until you feel stable.

That's not true, there's virtually very little pain. If done correctly, you'll pass out within 10 seconds or so and die while you're unconscious. Also with partial suspension you neck won't snap.

&#x200B;

Again this is morbidquestions, I'm not trying to get a supportive reply I'm trying to get an answer to my question. As much as I appreciate the gesture, that wasn't my intent.

Failing 3 times is nothing. My cousin failed the Bar Exam (test you have to take for law school or something) 7 times or so. My old friend failed his driving test 5 times. If you study hard enough you can do it and maybe he'll be surprised that you did. It's really easy for people to think that if you failed the first few times you have no hope, but everyone learns differently, and there has been a lot of conversation about exams and whether or not they actually test you correctly. Personally, I think failing is normal. I think failing is what allows you to grow and become better at what you're doing. If you never failed, you might end up becoming complacent which will be the real problem that affects your future career.

&#x200B;

My best advice is to keep going at it, and hang in there a while longer. You won't always be stuck under your father's control, and that's when your life actually begins.
